<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="dipyrromethane cofactor biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: DIPYRROMETHANE Dipyrromethane| is an active-site cofactor unique to |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.61|. The cofactor is elongated by the stepwise addition of four molecules of |FRAME: PORPHOBILINOGEN| through several intermediates (|FRAME: Hydroxymethylbilane-Synthase-ES ES|, |FRAME: Hydroxymethylbilane-Synthase-ES2 ES&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;| and |FRAME: Hydroxymethylbilane-Synthase-ES3 ES&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;|), generating an enzyme bound hexapyrrole (|FRAME: Hydroxymethylbilane-Synthase-ES4 ES&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;|). The final product, |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE|, is released from by a hydrolytic reaction that generates the &lt;i&gt;holo&lt;/i&gt;-deaminase with the cofactor still resident at the active site. The dipyrromethane cofactor is not found as a free compound. It arises from |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE| in a process that starts with the C-alkylation of an internal L-cysteine residue (Cys-261 in |FRAME: TAX-562|) of the &lt;i&gt;apo&lt;/i&gt;-deaminase by reaction with the 1-hydroxymethyl group of |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE|. This alkylation does not generate the cofactor but the |FRAME: Hydroxymethylbilane-Synthase-ES2 ES&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;| intermediate. Thus the cofactor is generated only after the enzyme has added two more pyrrole units to form the hexapyrrole, followed by release of |FRAME: HYDROXYMETHYLBILANE| |CITS: [8687374]|. The nucleotide sequences of genes encoding the deaminases from bacterial, plant and animal sources show considerable conservation in the deduced protein sequences, suggesting that all deaminases employ a similar mechanism to generate the cofactor |CITS: [8687374]|.</Description>
